why 2 1/4 LEAPS The tenor climbs C–D♭–E♭–F–G♭ by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 2 1/2 TENDENCY At the fermata the tenor leaps from G♭ down to C, the fifth of the F major chord. The G♭ belonged to the chord before; the leap takes the tenor to the note the fermata chord needs.
why 3 1/4 DOUBLING This passage is in B♭ minor, where G♭ is a normal note and this chord is iv⁶ (E♭ minor). Doubling the third of a minor chord is fine.
why 3 1/2 TENDENCY In B♭ minor, where the music is, G♭ is not chromatic. The alto rises G♭–A♭ and the bass drops to D for a diminished seventh chord; both move to that chord rather than resolving G♭.
why 5 3/4 DOUBLING This G♭ major chord is VI in B♭ minor (the checker reads it as a Neapolitan in F minor). Its root G♭ is doubled, as a root should be.
why 6 0 TENDENCY In B♭ minor the G♭ chord is VI, and its bass falls to D, the third of the B♭ major chord at the fermata. The checker reads a Neapolitan in F minor, which it is not here.
why 6 0 PROGRESSION Read in B♭ minor, this is VI moving through passing chords to B♭ major at the fermata. The checker hears F minor and calls it a step backward.
why 7 1/4 DOUBLING The music darkens toward A♭ minor, where C♭ is the third. Doubling the third of a minor chord is fine, and the C♭ in the bass gives the line its sorrowful slide.
why 7 1/2 TENDENCY The C♭ chord leads into a diminished seventh on G (G–B♭–D♭–F♭). The tenor rises C♭–D♭ and the bass drops to G for that chord: C♭ moves into the next sharp dissonance instead of falling.
why 10 0 SIX_FOUR Over the bass's F a B♭ minor chord sounds for one beat as the bass moves E♭–F–D♭–G♭. A passing ⁶₄ within a descending bass line.
why 10 1/2 DOUBLING Again the G♭ major chord, VI in B♭ minor, at the fermata, with its root G♭ doubled.
why 11 1/2 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to D♭ while the tenor steps C–D♭, so they meet in unison. Then the bass walks down D♭–C–B♭–A♭–G♭.
why 11 1/2 RANGE The leap takes the bass up to D♭ for one beat before it walks back down by step.
why 12 1/4 LEAPS As in bar 2, the tenor climbs C–D♭–E♭–F–G♭ by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 13 1/2 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to B♭ while the tenor resolves A–B♭, so they meet in unison.
why 13 3/4 DOUBLING As in bar 5: the G♭ major chord is VI in B♭ minor, with its root doubled.
why 14 0 TENDENCY As in bar 6: in B♭ minor the G♭ chord is VI, and its bass falls to D, the third of the B♭ major chord at the fermata.
why 14 0 PROGRESSION As in bar 6: in B♭ minor this is VI moving through passing chords to B♭ major, not a step backward.
why 15 1/4 TENDENCY The tune climbs G♭–A♭–B♭ as written. In B♭ minor G♭ is a normal step of the scale, free to rise.
why 15 1/2 DOUBLING The G♭ major chord, VI in B♭ minor, with its root doubled in alto and bass.
why 17 0 LEAPS The alto falls G♭–E♭–D♭–C into the final F major chord. Its ends form a tritone, but the line moves almost entirely by step.
